Zero of the Functions for Real Values of q.
See [20.36], section 2.8 for further results. Zero of ce_t_(x, q) and se_r_(z, q), Mc_c_m^(x, q), Ms_c_m^(x, q). In 0<=z<=x, ce_t_(z, q) and se_r_(z, q) have r real zeros. There are complex zeros if q>0. If z_0=x_0+iy_0 is any zero of ce_r_(z, q), se_r_(z, q) in
-\frac2<x_0<\frac2, then k\pi+z_0, k\pi+z_0
are also zeros, k an integer.

